GATE_EVIDENCE {"run":{"taskId":"c8656c70-d7a8-4864-88fa-c3a434a8d93b","passes":1},"task":[{"id":"c8656c70-d7a8-4864-88fa-c3a434a8d93b","department":"strategy","agent_id":"4aa67c8f-10b7-4599-bb73-372d57905213","task_class":"strategy","model_tier":"L1","status":"awaiting_approval","objective":"Decide the holding's strategy for its first continuous-revenue line next quarter: a paid Turkish-language newsletter on applied AI, or a B2B AI-automation service for small businesses. One decision, three reasons, the main risk, and the first week's single step; mark any figure you cannot source as a [placeholder].","result":{"text":"STRATEGY DECISION — First continuous-revenue line, next quarter\nSeat: Low Meadow Intake Specialist (corporate-development-analyst), strategy department\nTask row: c8656c70-d7a8-4864-88fa-c3a434a8d93b · project 23787153-fc24-47a8-b694-a88548c0393b · read back with queue_get · approval class on this row: internal\n\n1. THE DECISION\nBuild the B2B AI-automation service for small businesses first. Park the paid Turkish-language newsletter as a second line, to be reopened once the service has produced at least one signed, paid engagement.\n\nThink of it like opening a kitchen. A subscription magazine needs a printing press, a mailing list and a crowd before the first lira arrives; a catering job needs one customer who says yes to one dinner. We do not yet know how big our crowd is — so I am choosing the line that can be tested with one yes.\n\nI must be honest about the limits of this decision before the reasons: the holding has given me no prices, no cost of delivery, no cost of winning a customer, no audience size, no retention or cancellation data and no sales-cycle history for either line. Every one of those is a [placeholder] below. So this is not a claim that the service earns more, earns sooner, or is more profitable than the newsletter — I cannot measure any of that today. It is a claim about which line can be *proven or disproven cheapest*, which is a different and smaller claim.\n\n2. THREE REASONS\nReason one — the service can be validated by a single customer; the newsletter cannot.\nA service price is paid by one buyer at a time. A newsletter's economics depend on how many people subscribe, at what price, for how long — subscriber count [placeholder], price [placeholder], churn [placeholder]. One signed service engagement is a hard fact we would own; a newsletter needs an audience we have not counted before it tells us anything. This is a structural difference in the revenue model, not an assumption about size.\n\nReason two — the first thing we are missing is direct contact with paying buyers, and only the service produces it.\nNeither line can currently be priced, because the holding holds no record of what a Turkish small business will pay for applied AI — figure unavailable, [placeholder]. Service work puts us in the room with those buyers and leaves a written record of their processes and their quoted prices. I am NOT claiming that writing a newsletter would teach us nothing about delivery; I have no evidence either way, and the earlier draft that said so was wrong and is withdrawn. The narrower, supportable point is that selling a service generates buyer-contact records that both lines need, and the holding has none.\n\nReason three — the service is reversible at lower committed cost; the newsletter is a standing promise.\nA service engagement ends when its scope ends. A paid subscription is a promise of future issues on a schedule, and breaking it means refunds and reputational damage. Exact costs of either commitment: [placeholder]. If we are wrong about the service, we stop after the engagement; if we are wrong about the newsletter, we are still obliged to subscribers who have already paid.\n\nCORRECTION I OWE YOU on the service's shape. An earlier draft said a recurring automation service \"tolerates a slow week without breaking a promise.\" That was wrong and I withdraw it. If we charge a recurring monthly fee, the fee must buy an ongoing obligation — monitoring, fixing breakages, answering support requests — and a slow week on our side is then exactly as damaging as a missed newsletter issue. The workload and cost of that obligation are unmeasured: support hours per client per month [placeholder], failure rate [placeholder], cost to serve [placeholder]. Until they are measured, the first engagement should be sold as fixed-scope work with a separately priced, explicitly limited support window, not as an open-ended retainer. The decision above does not depend on the service being easier to run — only on it being cheaper to test.\n\n3. THE MAIN RISK\nThat nobody will pay. Demand and willingness to pay for AI automation among Turkish small businesses are not established anywhere in the material I was given — no prior sales, no quotes accepted, no price points, all [placeholder]. A business owner agreeing that a process is painful is not the same as that owner signing an invoice, and I will not treat a friendly conversation as evidence of a market. My earlier draft named \"custom work that cannot be resold\" as the risk that kills service businesses and dismissed lack of demand; that ranking was unsupported and is reversed here. Unvalidated demand is the main risk. The custom-work trap — every job rebuilt from nothing, no reusable asset — is the second risk, and it only matters if the first risk clears.\n\nHow we would know the main risk is real: an engagement that is offered at a stated price and refused, repeatedly, by buyers who agree the problem exists. That is the signal to stop and reopen the newsletter.\n\n4. THE FIRST WEEK — ONE STEP\nPrepare, in writing and for your signature, a single paid pilot offer: one page, one named candidate business drawn from contacts the holding already has, one narrowly defined repeated process, a fixed scope, a fixed price left as [placeholder] for you to set, and a stated, limited support window.\n\nNothing in that step leaves the holding. The offer is prepared, not sent. Setting the price, naming the client and sending anything outward are outward-facing acts that stop at your approval — I prepare them and never perform them. One point of accuracy: I verified only that this task row carries approval class \"internal\" (queue_get). I did not and cannot verify from here how outward acts are routed to your screen, so I will not promise that a future task appears on a dashboard automatically; what I can say is that the task carrying this offer must be filed with approval class \"outward\" so the decision lands with you, and I will say so in writing when I file it.\n\n5. WHAT THIS CHANGES FOR YOU THIS WEEK\nOne thing: you will be asked for one number — the pilot price — and one name. Nothing is spent, nothing is sent, and no commitment to subscribers exists. The newsletter is not cancelled; it is waiting behind one paying customer.\n\nUNSOURCED FIGURES, LISTED\nNewsletter: subscriber count, subscription price, churn/cancellation behaviour, viability threshold, production cost — all [placeholder].\nService: engagement price, recurring fee, cost to deliver, cost to acquire a customer, sales-cycle length, support hours, failure rate, number of clients needed to cover the line — all [placeholder].\nHolding: existing expertise in either line, existing contact list size, available working capital for next quarter — all [placeholder].\nNo figure above was estimated, recalled or carried over from any earlier conversation.","evidence":[{"kind":"verification","note":"Read back my own task row c8656c70-d7a8-4864-88fa-c3a434a8d93b: confirmed department strategy, approval_class 'internal', project_id 23787153-fc24-47a8-b694-a88548c0393b, and the exact objective/output_contract text the deliverable answers. This is also the only source for the approval-class statement in section 4 — no routing behaviour was claimed beyond it.","tool":"mcp__dxb-mcp__queue_get","toolCallId":"1636"}],"confidence":0.82,"acceptance_map":{"One decision":"Section 1 names a single choice — build the B2B AI-automation service first, park the Turkish-language newsletter behind one signed paid engagement — with no hedged alternative.","The main risk":"Section 3 names unvalidated demand and willingness to pay as the main risk, explicitly reverses the earlier draft's ranking that dismissed it, and states the observable signal that would prove it real.","Three reasons":"Section 2 gives exactly three numbered reasons: single-customer validation versus audience-dependent economics; the service produces buyer-contact records the holding lacks; lower committed cost and reversibility versus a standing subscription promise. Each is argued structurally, with no figure asserted.","The first week's single step":"Section 4 gives one step only — prepare, not send, a one-page fixed-scope paid pilot offer with price and client name left as [placeholders] for the CEO.","Mark any figure you cannot source as a [placeholder]":"No bare quantity appears anywhere in the deliverable. Every quantitative slot is bracketed at point of use, and section 'UNSOURCED FIGURES, LISTED' repeats all of them in one register, with an explicit statement that nothing was estimated or recalled.","Undefined specifics marked as explicit [placeholders]":"Prices, client name, fee levels, audience size, churn, cost to serve, sales-cycle length, acquisition cost, capital and existing expertise are all marked [placeholder]; no name, date or price is invented.","Gate objection 2 and 7 (unsupported learning asymmetry)":"Reason three of the earlier draft is removed. Reason two now explicitly withdraws the claim that newsletter writing teaches nothing about delivery, states that no evidence exists either way, and narrows the claim to the one supportable point: the service generates buyer-contact records the holding does not hold.","Gate objection 8 (unverified automatic approval routing)":"Section 4 states only what queue_get verified — this row carries approval class 'internal' — and says in plain words that routing of future outward acts to the CEO's screen was not verified and is not promised; instead the commitment is to file the offer's task with approval class 'outward' and say so in writing.","Gate objection 4 (custom-work trap wrongly ranked above demand)":"Section 3 reverses the ranking explicitly: unvalidated demand is the main risk, custom work is named as the second risk that only matters once the first clears, and the deliverable states that a friendly conversation is not evidence of paid demand.","Gate objection 6 (a handful of customers covers the line / service pays rent sooner)":"Both claims are deleted. Section 1 adds an explicit disclaimer that no claim is made about more revenue, faster revenue or profitability, because prices, delivery cost, acquisition cost, audience size and sales-cycle evidence are all absent and listed as [placeholders].","Gate objection 3 and 9 (recurring revenue tolerates a slow week; ongoing obligations never established)":"The 'CORRECTION I OWE YOU' block withdraws that sentence by name, states that a recurring fee must buy an ongoing obligation, marks support hours, failure rate and cost to serve as [placeholders], and changes the recommended first engagement to fixed-scope with a limited, separately priced support window rather than an open retainer.","Gate objection 1 and 5 (unsourced quantities: thousands of readers, one hand, two issues, three clients, five clients)":"Every one of those claims is deleted. 'Thousands of paying readers', 'countable on one hand', 'cancellation after two issues', 'three clients' and 'at most five clients' do not appear. The decision is rebuilt on model structure, and section 1 states plainly that no claim is made about which line earns more or sooner."}},"feedback":null,"created_at":"2026-10-09T21:00:16.284Z","updated_at":"2026-10-09T21:03:30.137Z"}],"events":[{"event":"created","from_status":"inbox","to_status":"queued","actor":"orchestrator:dispatch","payload":{"deps":[],"envelope_index":0},"created_at":"2026-10-09T21:00:16.284Z"},{"event":"claimed","from_status":"queued","to_status":"claimed","actor":"b51-gate-live","payload":{"lease_seconds":900},"created_at":"2026-10-09T21:00:16.299Z"},{"event":"transition","from_status":"claimed","to_status":"running","actor":"b51-gate-live","payload":{},"created_at":"2026-10-09T21:00:16.306Z"},{"event":"transition","from_status":"running","to_status":"review","actor":"b51-gate-live","payload":{"confidence":0.82},"created_at":"2026-10-09T21:03:06.175Z"},{"event":"transition","from_status":"review","to_status":"awaiting_approval","actor":"orchestrator:qa","payload":{"qa":"pass","notes":"All contract elements present and self-contained: one unambiguous decision (build B2B AI-automation service first, park newsletter), exactly three numbered reasons, a single named main risk (unvalidated demand/willingness to pay, with second risk explicitly subordinated), and one first-week step (prepare, not send, a one-page fixed-scope pilot offer). Every quantitative slot — prices, subscriber count, churn, cost to serve, acquisition cost, sales cycle, support hours, capital — is bracketed as [placeholder] at point of use and re-listed in a closing register; client name and price left as [placeholder] rather than invented. No fabricated names, dates or figures detected. Minor deductions only for style, not contract: internal meta-noise (seat/task-row IDs, \"CORRECTION I OWE YOU\", references to withdrawn earlier drafts) clutters a CEO-facing deliverable, and the reasoning is deliberately narrowed to \"cheapest to test\" rather than revenue comparison — defensible given the absent data, and it does not breach the contract.","judge_ms":23958,"confidence":0.82,"judge_attempts":1},"created_at":"2026-10-09T21:03:30.142Z"}],"runs":[{"id":"6a172a30-1de0-4fd0-a0b2-919865feafce","employee_id":"4aa67c8f-10b7-4599-bb73-372d57905213","task_id":"c8656c70-d7a8-4864-8