arking them as [placeholders].","severity":"high"},{"why":"The context provides no evidence about the holding’s expertise, either activity’s learning benefits, or their costs. This unsupported asymmetry supplies an entire decision reason.","from":"Sol 6.1","claim":"Service experience necessarily makes a later newsletter cheaper and better, while newsletter writing teaches almost nothing about delivery.","severity":"medium"},{"why":"The answer proposes monthly client fees but justifies resilience by treating delivery as project work. It never establishes the ongoing obligations. Maintenance, failures, and support can require timely work; their workload and cost are absent from the comparison.","from":"Sol 6.1","claim":"A recurring automation service tolerates a slow week without breaking a promise.","severity":"high"},{"why":"Nothing establishes demand or willingness to pay for the proposed automation. Free diagnostic conversations identify repeated problems but do not establish paid demand, so excluding that risk from the decision is unsupported.","from":"Sol 6.1","claim":"The custom-work trap is the risk that kills service businesses, “not lack of demand.”","severity":"medium"},{"why":"The answer asserts that a newsletter requires thousands of paying readers, the service needs at most five clients for equivalent revenue, and subscribers cancel after two missed issues. None is sourced or marked [placeholder]. These are consequential numerical assumptions, contradicting both the contract and the acceptance map.","from":"Astra 6","claim":"Every unsourced quantity is marked as a [placeholder].","severity":"high"},{"why":"The context supplies no prices, delivery costs, acquisition costs, audience size, or sales-cycle evidence. Higher assumed revenue per customer does not establish profitability or faster revenue. Placeholder prices do not substantiate this central comparison.","from":"Astra 6","claim":"A handful of service customers covers the line, and the service pays